        <div class="card" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem">
            Cooked vs uncooked rice calories: what is the difference?
          </h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:0.75rem">
            One of the most common mistakes when calculating rice calories is mixing up cooked
            and uncooked weights. Dry rice contains much less water, so its calories per 100g
            are considerably higher than those of cooked rice.
          </p>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:0.75rem">
            During cooking, rice absorbs water and increases in weight. For example, 100g of
            uncooked white rice may provide around 360 kcal and produce roughly 250–300g of
            cooked rice, depending on the variety and cooking method. The total calories in the
            rice itself remain broadly similar; the extra weight comes mainly from water.
          </p>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8">
            If you track calories, weigh rice in the same state as the nutritional information
            you are using. Dry weights are often easier to compare with package labels, while
            cooked values are useful when weighing food that is already prepared. For recipes
            containing oil, stock or sauces, remember to count those ingredients separately.
          </p>
